#0
ACTION-QUEUE QUEUE

	n
	YAction Queue	n
	n
Usage: queue, queue clear

An action's type essentially tells you how long the action takes to perform
(within the framework of the 6-second combat round) and how movement is treated.
Although primarily critical in combat situations, the action queue is always in
effect.  An example includes opening a door, which will always consume a move-
action regardless of whether you are in combat or not.

Only commands with a non-mutating availability check can enter the action queue.
If a command cannot be checked safely in advance, retry it after the required
action becomes available.
	n
This representation can be found in your prompt in the form of:
[smw]
S = Standard Action
M = Move Action
W = Swift Action
	n
	cStandard Action	n
In combat, a standard action represents doing a maneuver that takes the same
amount of time as your normal combat rotation.  An example is performing a
feint.
	n
	cMove Action	n
A move action is a brief maneuver that can be performed along with a single
melee attack in a round.  An example is opening a door.
	n
	cSwift Action	n
A swift action is an extremely brief maneuver that doesn't not interfere
with combat.  The main difference between a swift and free action is that
you can't perform more than one swift action in a given round.  An example
is casting a spell affected by the metamagic quickened feat.
	n
	cFree Actions	n
A free action is any sort of action that doesn't not use any of your action
points.  Essentially the maneuver is so effortless that you can perform it
without any sort of effect on the action system.
	n
	YSee also:	n 	Ractions	n, 	Rattack queue	n, 	Rcombat	n
	n
#0
ACTIONS ACTIONS-IN-COMBAT SMW

	n
	YActions In Combat	n
	n
During one turn, there are a wide variety of actions that your character can perform, from
swinging a sword to casting a spell.
	n
	yTypes of Combat Options	n
	n
Many attacks are basic combat options or combat maneuvers any character can attempt, while
others are available only through attack-oriented feats. Different combat options require
different types of actions. The action type defines which options can be used together.
	n
	cBasic:	n Anyone can use these combat options, including charging and fighting defensively.
	n
	cCombat Maneuvers:	n Combat maneuvers are a specific set of basic options that use your
Combat Maneuver Bonus and Combat Maneuver Defense. There are several combat maneuvers
(disarm, grapple, trip, etc).
	n
	cFeats:	n Numerous feats grant additional combat options, such as Cleave and Power Attack.
Each feat defines the circumstances in which it can be used. Characters without these feats
can't attempt the special attacks detailed in those feats.
	n
	yAction Types	n
	n
An action's type essentially tells you how long the action takes to perform (within the
framework of the 6-second combat round).
	n
	cThere are six types of actions:	n
	n
Standard
Move
Full-round
Swift
Immediate
Free
	n
In a normal round, you can perform a standard action and a move action, or you can perform a
full-round action. You can also perform one swift action and one or more free actions. You
can always take a move action in place of a standard action.
	n
	YSee also:	n 	Rcombat maneuver	n, 	Rattack queue	n, 	Raction queue	n
	n
