#0
ANSI COLOR-CODES COLOR-LEVEL COLORCODES COLORCODING COLORS COLOUR-CODES COLOURCODES COLOURS PCOLORS

	n 
	nThe special character used to indicate the start of a colour sequence is 
	n'	*' (i.e. a SINGLE at-sign).  When you start a color code you must
	nterminate the color with the color code 'n' to stop bleeding.
	n
	n The predefined colours are:
	n
	n   n: no colour (switches colour off)
	n 
	n   	rr: dark red	n                        	RR: light red	n
	n   	gg: dark green	n                      	GG: light green	n
	n   	bb: dark blue	n                       	BB: light blue	n
	n   	yy: dark yellow	n                     	YY: light yellow	n
	n   	mm: dark magenta	n                    	MM: light magenta	n
	n   	cc: dark cyan	n                       	CC: light cyan	n
	n   	ww: dark white	n                      	WW: light white	n
	n 
	n   	aa: dark azure	n                      	AA: light azure	n
	n   	jj: dark jade	n                       	JJ: light jade	n
	n   	ll: dark lime	n                       	LL: light lime	n
	n   	oo: dark orange	n                     	OO: bright orange	n
	n   	pp: dark pink	n                       	PP: light pink	n
	n   	tt: dark tan	n                        	TT: light tan	n
	n   	vv: dark violet	n                     	VV: light violet	n
	n   	dd: dark grey/black	n                 	DD: light grey	n
	n   	__: underlined (if supported)	n       	++: bold (if supported)	n
	n   	--: blinking (if supported)	n         	==: reverse (if supported)	n
	n   *: at-sign	n
	n 
	n   	11: base palette 1	n                  	22: base palette 2	n
	n   	13: base palette 3	n
	n 
	nIt's also possible to explicitly specify an RGB value, by including the four 
	ncharacter colour sequence (as used by ColourRGB) within square brackets, eg:
	n 
	n    This is a 	*[F010]very dark green foreground	*n.
	n              	[F010]very dark green foreground	n
	n
	nBUILDERS:
	n
	nFinally, this function also allows you to embed MXP tags.  The easiest and 
	nsafest way to do this is via the ( and ) bracket options:
	n    
	n    From here, you can walk 	*(north	*).
	n
	nHowever it's also possible to include more explicit MSP tags, like this:
	n 
	n    The baker offers to sell you a 	*<send href="buy pie">pie	*</send>.
	n                                   	<send href="buy pie">pie	</send>
	n 
	nNote that the MXP tags will automatically be removed if the user doesn't 
	nsupport MXP, but it's very important you remember to close the tags.
	n
	nCOLOR SETTINGS:
	n
	nYou can change your character's color settings via PREFEDIT.
	n
See also: 	*, PROMPT, TOGGLE
Staff: SHOW COLOUR
#0
COMBAT COMBAT-PHASE FIGHTING

The usual way to gain experience in Luminari is via combat.  Combat in Luminari 
is multi-layered and is designed for a fast-paced, active experience.  
 
Initiating Combat:
Combat can be initiated in a couple ways.  The most usual way to initate combat 
is to use the kill or hit commands : kill <name> or hit <name>.  This will 
initiate combat between you and the specified mobile or player.  If
you have a Combat Maneuver (CM) in the attack queue, that CM will be used 
instead of a melee attack.
Combat may also be initiated via a CM, trip for example : trip <name>.  This 
command will initiate combat with a trip CM.
 
Combat Rounds:
The combat round lasts 6 seconds, divided into 3 2-second phases.  The attacks 
in your attack rotation (help ATTACKS) are divided up between these phases in 
the following way:
 
Phase 1: attacks 1, 4, 7
Phase 2: attacks 2, 5, 8
Phase 3: attacks 3, 6, 9
 
You can check the number of attacks you have in your rotation by using the ATTACKS 
command.  The number of attacks available is directly based on your Base Attack
Bonus (BAB), which increases based on your class and level.
 
Actions in Combat:
Combat is directly affected by the actions you have available.  If you have 
both a standard action and a move action available, you will automatically 
perform a full attack every combat round, utilizing your entire attack rotation.
If you have only a standard action, then only attacks in the first phase will be 
performed.  If your character has neither a move nor a standard action, no 
attacks will be made in this round.  See 'help ACTIONS' for more information.
 
Combat Modes:
There are a number of combat modes available that change the way you fight.  
Some modes are always available and some are unlocked by feats.  Knowing when to
use the different modes is vital for mastering combat.  see 'help COMBAT-MODES'
for more information.
 
Attacks of Opportunity:
Sometimes a combatant in a melee lets his guard down or takes a reckless action. 
In this case, combatants engaged to this combatant can take advantage of his 
lapse in defense to attack him for free. These free attacks are called attacks 
of opportunity, and are marked 	W[	RAOO	W]	n next to the attack.  Usually, you
are able to make one Attack of opportunity (AOO) every 6 seconds.  If you have
the Combat Reflexes feat, however, you may make a number of Attacks of 
opportunity equal to your dexterity bonus every 6 seconds. See 'help AAO' for
more information
 
Combat Maneuvers:
During combat, you can attempt to perform a number of maneuvers that can hinder 
or even cripple your foe.  Many of these attacks can be used in place of a 
melee attack, allowing you to combine different combat maneuvers together to 
stack bonuses on your allies or penalties on your enemies.  Some maneuvers may
trigger an AOO from your opponents.  See 'help COMBAT-MANEUVERS' for more 
information.
 
See Also: COMBAT-MANEUVERS, ATTACK-QUEUE, ACTION-QUEUE, ACTIONS, COMBAT-MODES, AOO
#0
COMBAT-MANEUVER


Please see:
	Rhelp combat maneuvers	n
	Rhelp combat maneuver check	n
	Rhelp maneuvers	n

#0
COMBAT-MANEUVER-BONUS COMBAT-MANEUVER-CHECK COMBAT-MANEUVER-DEFENSE

	n
	RCombat Maneuver Bonus	n
	n
Each character and creature has a Combat Maneuver Bonus (or CMB) that
represents its skill at performing combat maneuvers. A creature's CMB
is determined using the following formula:
	n
	YCMB = Base attack bonus + Strength modifier + special size modifier	n
	n
Some feats and abilities grant a bonus to your CMB when performing
specific maneuvers.
	n
	RCombat Maneuver Defense	n
	n
Each character and creature has a Combat Maneuver Defense (or CMD)
that represents its ability to resist combat maneuvers. A creature's
CMD is determined using the following formula:
	n
	YCMD = 10 + Base attack bonus + Strength modifier + Dexterity modifier +	n
	Yspecial size modifier + miscellaneous modifiers	n
	n
Some feats and abilities grant a bonus to your CMB or CMD when
resisting specific maneuvers.
	n
	RMiscellaneous Modifiers	n
	n
A creature can also add any circumstance, deflection, dodge, insight,
luck, morale, profane, and sacred bonuses to AC to its CMD. Any
penalties to a creature's AC also apply to its CMD. A flat-footed
creature does not add its Dexterity bonus to its CMD.
	n
	RDetermine Success	n
	n
If your attack roll equals or exceeds the CMD of the target, your
maneuver is a success and has the listed effect. Some maneuvers, such
as bull rush, have varying levels of success depending on how much your
attack roll exceeds the target's CMD. Rolling a natural 20 while
attempting a combat maneuver is always a success (except when
attempting to escape from bonds), while rolling a natural 1 is always
a failure.
	n
	YSee Also:	n Special-Size-Modifier	n
	n
#0
COMBAT-MANEUVERS


During combat, you can perform a number of maneuvers that can
hinder or even cripple an opponent.  Although these maneuvers
are vastly different, they use similar mechanic to determine
success.

You can use the "maneuvers" command to see which maneuvers are currently available to you.

Examples of combat maneuvers:
intimidate
disarm
feint
shieldslam
shieldcharge
springleap
dirtkick
sap
guard
headbutt
circle
hitall
rescue
treatinjury
taunt
disengage
backstab
kick
trip
bash
shieldpunch
charge
grapple

Notice: Some of the combat maneuvers require feats/class to acquire

Please read: 	Rhelp combat maneuver check	n

#0
COMBAT-MODE COMBAT-MODES COMBATMODE COMBATMODES

In Luminari, certain feats and classes allow the use of 'combat modes', 
a mode where the char gains certain bonuses and have certain penalties. 
A good example of this is power attack - Gain a bonus in damage by      
taking a penalty to you attacks.  These modes can be changed during     
combat, increasing the versatility of the fighter classes and providing 
additional options for the fighting man.

	YSee also:	n COMBAT, POWER-ATTACK
#0
COMBAT-PROMPT

28810/28810H 5000/5000V >
<T: Zusuk TC: perfect> <E: a target dummy EC: bad>

The first line will display your prompt settings (PREFEDIT).
In this example, it is showing your Hit-Points and Move-Points.

The second line is your combat display.
T:  This is the tank's name
TC:  This is the tank's current condition
E:  This is the enemy that you are currently targeting
EC:  This is the enemy's current condition

See Also:  PREFEDIT

#0
COMBATROLL

CombatRoll

Usage: combatroll

This is a toggle that will turn off and on details about combat.  It is
suggested you leave this toggle off unless you are an advanced player.

This is also available to turn off and on in the prefedit menu.

#0
COME-AND-GET-ME COMEANDGETME

This ability will allow the Berserker, if raging, to sacrifice defense for offense.
The Berserker will be prone to being hit more often but in turn have the
opportunity to strike very powerfully in return.

Usage: comeandgetme
