CURSE-TOUCH CURSETOUCH

This ability will allow the Cleric to curse their target.
You must be able to touch the target to deliver the curse.

Usage: cursetouch

SEE ALSO: curse
#0
D-C DIFFICULTY-CLASS

The difficulty-class represents an arbitrary value, relative to the situation - the
more challenging the situation, the higher the value.  Then either a skill, stat or
ability added to a diceroll is compared to the difficulty-class.  If you beat the
difficulty class, then you overcome the situation.

Example:  Fleeing in combat has a D-C of 15, otherwise the opponent(s) will get a free
attack on you.  And fleeing is compared to your TUMBLE ability.  So assuming you have a
TUMBLE rating of 5:
Tumble (5) + DiceRoll 1d20 (rolled a 7) = 12 vs D-C of 15
therefore you failed and
your opponent will get a free attack on you.

See Also:  FLEE TUMBLE
#0
DAMAGE-REDUCTION

Damage Reduction refers to some protection players and mobiles have
to completely ignore the first X points of all incoming damaging
attacks.

See Also:  SKILL-
#0
DAMAGE-TYPE DAMAGE-TYPE-RESISTANCE DAMAGE-TYPE-VULNERABILITY

All forms of damage your character can possibly suffer have a "type"
category.  Some races have vulnerabilities/strengths versus various
damage-types.  Some spells can offer resistance to certain damage-
types.

When you type 'affect' you can see your damage resistances.
The first value followed by the % symbol indicates a percentage of the
damage from that elemental type that you will absorb.  The second value
which is surrounded by () symbols indicate the amount of damage reduction
you have against that element.

See also:  AFFECT-COMMAND

#0
DAMAGE-ROLL DAMAGEROLL DAMROLL ROLL

   A bonus added to your armor and weapons that allows your character to hit 
things harder and with a stronger force.
   A player at level fifteen with a Damroll +15 will do more damage to a 
creature, than a level fifteen player would do with a Damroll of +10.

See also: HITROLL, AC, STATS
