#0
FATIGUE

Fatigue is a negative effect often caused by magical spells.  Fatigue
will cause the victim to not gain any movement points via normal
regeneration and will additionally often drain some of the victim's
current movement-points.

Also due to the artificial exhaustion, the victim will suffer
penalties to their hitroll, damroll and armor-class.

See Also:  SPELLS
#0
FAVORED-ENEMY

Usage:  Automatic
Special:  Use the 'study' command to modify the list

'Favored enemy' is a ranger ability that indicates the ranger's
chosen list of hunted victims.  It is based on race and gives a
+2 bonus to hitroll, damage and armor-class against the chosen
opponent(s).  In addition, this bonus will increase by another
+1 for every 5 levels achieved in the ranger class.

Type 'study ranger' to alter the list of enemies, it can only
be modified once per level gained as a ranger.

See Also:  STUDY RANGER
#0
FEAR

While under the affect/effect fear, there is a good chance every round
you spend in combat, that you will flee in terror.

See also:  SCARE
#0
FEAT-INFO

Usage: feat info <featname>

Display detailed information about a particular feat.  

See also: FEATS FEAT-SYSTEM
#0
FEAT-SYSTEM

 
A feat is a special feature that either gives your character a new 
capability or improves one he or she already has.  Unlike skills, a 
feat has no ranks - You either have a feat or you do not.  While your 
class is the basic starting point for your abilities, feats help you 
overcome class limitations, customize your character and increase your 
power.  
 
Feats are highly varied and can confer many different types of 
advantages.  A feat could slightly improve your combat effectiveness, 
alter your spells or grant a bonus on saving throws in a particular 
situation.  Feats also determine your proficiency with weapons, 
shields and armor.
 
Feat progression for non-epic levels is every 3 levels (3, 6, 9, 12, etc)

See Also: COMBAT-FEATS METAMAGIC-FEATS SPELLCASTING-FEATS GENERAL-FEATS

#0
FEATLISTING

This command will print a list of all feats including their corresponding number.
#0
FEATS

NOTE: This is the help topic for the 'feats' command, for general 
information about the Luminari Feat system, please see 'help 
FEAT-SYSTEM'.

Usage: feats <all|available|known> <description>
       feat info <featname>

The feats command displays information in varying levels of detail
regarding your current feat selection.  The feats command, when used
with no arguments, will list your currently selected feats.  
'feats all' will list all feats in the feat system, regardless of their 
availability.  'feats available' will list all feats available to you
based on the prerequisites of each feat.  'feats known' lists all 
selected feats, just like the feats command.

'feats all', feats available' and 'feats known' can all be used with 
the additional 'description' parameter to display a short message
describing each feat. (e.g 'feats known desc' will display all known
feats and their short descriptions.)

The feat info command is used to get fine grained information about 
a particular feat.  The prerequisites are color coded - You do not 
fulfill prerequisites colored in red.

See also: FEAT-SYSTEM FEAT-INFO
#0
FEATSET

Featset is a staff command used to assign/remove specific feats from players.

Syntax: featset <target name> '<feat name>' <# of this feat to add or subtract>

See also:  abilityset, skillset
