#0
WEAPON-ABILITY-AGILE WEAPON-AGILE

   An agile weapon will use the wielder's dex bonus in place of strength bonus
when determining damage bonus.  This bonus will be capped by the weapon's
enhancement bonus.  The cap does not apply to strength, in the event that the
wielder's strength is higher than their dex.
#0
WEAPON-CREATION

   When you create weapons, try to help round out the distribution of weapon
types. What I mean by this is that there are tons of swords already in the
game, but not that many pole arms or flails. You are probably going to get a
few groans from the immortals if you create yet another sword while neglecting
the other weapon types. No weapon may ever exceed 20 pounds in weight and do not
forget backstabbing is only possible with piercing weapons.
   So often builders always make the biggest and baddest weapons with little
consideration of reality. Before you make a weapon, think about who will be
using it, how much should it weigh, how much damage should it inflict. Use
common sense. A two-handed battleaxe should always do more damage than a 
dagger. Otherwise, why would I be a warrior and able to use large weapons if
I can be a rogue and use a dagger that does more damage? Warriors should have
the best weapons. Wizards should have the weakest. Weight is important enough
to mention again. A weight of 18, means only a player with strength 18 or up
can wield it, perfect to make weapons restricted to only warriors, and it also
makes a mage with the strength spell very important. Each race and class has
their advantages and disadvantages, use them.
   Some permanent effects such as sanctuary and armor should be reserved for
armor not weapons, basically weapons would most often benefit the wielder with
some sort of detection spell, not protection. Also remember that some classes
should not use certain weapons, so weapons have to be within limits, or the 
game will be unbalanced.

See also: SWORDS, DAMAGE
#0
WEAPON-ABILITIES WEAPON-AFFECTS WEAPON-EFFECTS WEAPON-SPECIAL-ABILITIES

Some weapons have special abilities that come into affect via various methods, though
usually when scoring a hit or a critical hit.  Sometimes the weapon's special ability
can be toggled on and off using the command 'utter'. Each weapon may have a different
command word.  For example a flaming weapon may have the command word: blaze.  So to toggle
on and off the flaming effect, you'd type 'utter blaze'.

Seeking      - Will hit through concealment, ranged weapons only 
Adaptive     - Will apply full strength bonus to damage, ranged weapons only
Disruption   - Deals extra damage on undead on hit and critical. 
Defending    - Applies 1/2 enhancement bonus to AC, and reduces enhancement bonus to hit and damage by 1/2
Exhausting   - Inflicts Fatigue on critical
Bane         - Deals extra damage against a specified racial type
Corrosive    - Deals extra acid damage
Speed        - Gives extra attack each round (does not stack with haste) 
Ghost Touch  - Hit incorporeal targets as if they were not incorporeal 
Blinding     - Inflicts blindness on critical
Shock        - Deals extra electricity damage
Flaming      - Deals extra fire damage
Thundering   - Deals sonic damage on crit with chance for deafness 
Agile        - Deals dexterity bonus in place of strength bonus on weapon hit, capped at weapon enhancement bonus
Wounding     - Causes bleeding on hit
Lucky        - Adds 1/2 enhancement bonus on most d20 rolls 
Bewildering  - Inflicts confuse on critical
Keen         - Doubles the base threat range on an item.  Does not stack with improved critical feat
Vicious      - Inflicts large damage on hit, but also inflicts small damage on wielder
Invigorating - Restores stamina with each hit
Vorpal       - Small chance on a critical to kill the target outright (won't work on undead, constructs or oozes)
Vampiric     - Small amount of hp healed with each hit

