Removes clan claims for the specified zone.  Does not affect popularity.

See Also: CLAN-CLAIM POPULARITY SHOW-CLAIMS SHOW-POPULARITY

#32
CLANSET

Usage: clanset
       clanset <clan ID> <field> <value>

Imp-level command to manually set almost any aspect of the clan.  Typing clanset
with no arguments will display a list of available fields.

See Also: CLAN-EDIT

#32
PERFMON

Usage: perfmon [all | summ | prof | mem | slow | sql | entities | combat |
                saves | top | csv | reset | sect]

Staff performance and memory monitoring tool.

Subcommands:
  perfmon all                  - Print the decision-oriented health report.
  perfmon summ                 - Print pulse usage and latency summary.
  perfmon prof                 - Print cumulative profiling statistics.
  perfmon mem                  - Print memory history and entity inventory.
  perfmon slow [count] [csv]   - Print recent over-budget pulse records.
  perfmon sql [csv]            - Print SQL ownership and latency telemetry.
  perfmon entities [csv]       - Print entity lifecycle and registry telemetry.
  perfmon combat [count] [csv] - Print slow combat and chain-limit telemetry.
  perfmon saves                - Print incremental persistence state and costs.
  perfmon top <metric> [limit] - Rank sections by total, max, or p99.
  perfmon csv                  - Output all telemetry in machine-readable format.
  perfmon reset                - Start a new monitoring window.
  perfmon sect <section>       - Print one profiling section.

Percentiles cover bounded recent samples; totals, averages, and maxima are cumulative
since reset. Persistence work is dirty-driven and spread across pulses. Slow-pulse
records correlate schedule classes, sections, SQL, events, entities, and missed-pulse
replay without retaining SQL values, commands, credentials, or player text.

Before each accepted copyover, the server atomically overwrites
log/perfmon-pre-copyover.txt with the complete current report. Only the newest snapshot
is retained; a failed snapshot leaves the prior complete file intact and does not stop
the copyover.

